direction: down

title: |md
  ### Inside one eval run: phases, not a per-question loop
|

card: GPU (8 GB) {
  style.stroke-dash: 3
  style.fill: "#f6f8fa"

  p1: "PHASE 1 - retrieve" {
    style.fill: "#d4edda"
    embed: "one embeddings batch for the whole set (bge-m3)"
    search: "hybrid search per question, wide pool when reranking"
    embed -> search
  }

  p2: "PHASE 2 - rerank" {
    style.fill: "#fff3cd"
    flat: "flatten to pairs: 100 questions x 20 candidates = 2000"
    once: "one predict for all of them, fp16 on the card"
    back: "slice scores back per question, keep top k"
    flat -> once -> back
  }

  p3: "PHASE 3 - generate" {
    style.fill: "#d4edda"
    gen: "answer from the context that is already prepared\n(llama3.1:8b)"
    logrow: "question_log + config snapshot: rerank, device, k, phased"
    gen -> logrow
  }

  p1 -> p2: "ollama models unloaded (keep_alive 0)" {
    style.stroke-width: 2
  }
  p2 -> p3: "reranker unloaded, cuda cache emptied" {
    style.stroke-width: 2
  }
}

why: |md
  **Why phases.** Per question the loop needed the embedder, then the
  reranker, then the generator. Together they do not fit in 8 GB, so
  ollama evicted and reloaded a model on every single question.
  Phases make each stage own the card alone: two swaps per run instead
  of two per question, and the reranker gets a real batch
  (44 s for 100 questions on the card, about 16 min on CPU).

  Interactive answers stay a straight line: one question cannot be
  batched, and the agent decides for itself when to search.
|
